      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square",
      "problem": "A circle in the Cartesian plane is given by the equation \n$137+6y=-{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-24x.$ If the circle is written in the standard form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2},$ then $a+b+r=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the standard form of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}137+6y & =-{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-24x \\\\ {x}^{2}+24x+{y}^{2}+6y & =-137 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+24x)+({y}^{2}+6y) & =-137 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 12x+{12}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}) & =-137+{12}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 12x+{12}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+12)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-12))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={4}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-12,b=-3$ and $r=4,$  and\n\n$$\na+b+r=-12-3+4=-11.\n$$",
