      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle by Completing the Square with Fractions",
      "problem": "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}-4x+2{y}^{2}-y=-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x+2{y}^{2}-y & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+({y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}+{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{16} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(1,\\frac{1}{4})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{4}.$",
